ENTRY 12477 {"id":12477,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":" */\nexport class ProcessHandle {\n  readonly pid: number;\n\n  private consumer: \"none\" | \"callbacks\" | \"iterator\" | \"drain\" = \"none\";\n  private closed = false;\n  private terminal = false;\n  private exitSeen = false;\n  private streamEnded = false;\n  /** Set by the iterator's `return()`, i.e. when a `for await` loop is left. */\n  private iteratorReleased = false;\n  private pullChain: Promise<unknown> = Promise.resolve();\n  private readonly exit: Promise<{ exitCode: number }>;\n  private resolveExit!: (result: { exitCode: number }) => void;\n  private rejectExit!: (err: unknown) => void;\n  private iterator?: AsyncIterableIterator<ProcessEvent>;\n  private unwatch?: () => void;\n\n  /** @internal */\n  constructor(\n    private readonly session: ProcessSession,\n    private readonly callbacks: Pick<StartOptions, \"onStdout\" | \"onStderr\">,\n  ) {\n    this.pid = session.pid;\n    this.exit = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n      this.resolveExit = resolve;\n      this.rejectExit = reject;\n    });\n    // A caller that only iterates `events` never awaits this.\n    this.exit.catch(() => {});\n    // Reads are what normally surface a failure, but nothing reads once the\n    // caller stops iterating, so cancellation must settle `exit` on its own.\n    this.unwatch = session.onAbort((err) => this.fail(err));\n    if (callbacks.onStdout || callbacks.onStderr) {\n      this.consumer = \"callbacks\";\n      void this.pump();\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The process's events in order, ending with one \"exit\" event. Only\n   * available if no callbacks were given to `start()` and `wait()` has not\n   * taken over. Iterating is resumable: leaving a `for await` loop early\n   * neither kills the process nor discards anything, until `wait()` is\n   * called, which then discards the rest of the output.\n   */\n  get events(): AsyncIterable<ProcessEvent> {\n    if (this.consumer !== \"none\" && this.consumer !== \"iterator\") {\n      throw invalidArgument(\n        \"events is unavailable: output is already consumed by start() callbacks or wait()\",\n      );\n    }\n    this.consumer = \"iterator\";\n    if (!this.iterator) {\n      const iterator: AsyncIterableIterator<ProcessEvent> = {\n        [Symbol.asyncIterator]: () => iterator,\n        next: async () => {\n          if (this.consumer === \"drain\") {\n            throw invalidArgument(\n              \"events is unavailable: wait() took over consuming the output\",\n            );\n          }\n          this.iteratorReleased = false;\n          const step = await this.pull();\n          return step.done ? { done: true, value: undefined } : step;\n        },\n        // Called when a `for await` loop is left early. The process keeps\n        // running and nothing is discarded, but the stream is no longer read,\n        // so a later wait() has to read it.\n        return: async () => {\n          this.iteratorReleased = true;\n          return { done: true, value: undefined };\n        },\n      };\n      this.iterator = iterator;\n    }\n      return { done: true, value: undefined };\n    }\n    try {\n      const step = await this.session.next();\n      if (step.done) {\n        this.streamEnded = true;\n        return step;\n      }\n      if (step.value.type === \"exit\") {\n        this.exitSeen = true;\n        this.markTerminal();\n        this.resolveExit({ exitCode: step.value.exitCode });\n      }\n      return step;\n    } catch (err) {\n      if (this.exitSeen) {\n        // A failure while draining the stream's tail, after a clean exit,\n        // changes nothing the caller was promised.\n        this.streamEnded = true;\n        return { done: true, value: undefined };\n      }\n      const failure = this.closed\n        ? new SandboxClosedError(\"Process handle is closed\")\n        : err;\n      this.fail(failure);\n      throw failure;\n    }\n  }\n\n  private async pump(): Promise<void> {\n    try {\n      while (true) {\n        const step = await this.pull();\n        if (step.done) return;\n        const event = step.value;\n        if (event.type === \"stdout\") this.callbacks.onStdout?.(event.data);\n        else if (event.type === \"stderr\") this.callbacks.onStderr?.(event.data);\n      }\n    } catch (err) {\n      // pull() settles `exit` itself for a stream failure, so reaching here\n      // with it still pending means a user callback threw.\n      if (!this.terminal) {\n        this.closed = true;\n        // Settle with the callback's error first: cancelling below also\n        // reports a \"closed\" failure through onAbort().\n        this.fail(err);\n        this.session.cancel();\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Public facade for `sandbox.commands.*`. Obtain an instance via\n * `sandbox.commands`; do not construct directly.\n */\nexport class SandboxCommands {\n  /** @internal */\n  constructor(private readonly ops: CommandsOperations) {}\n\n  /**\n   * Executes `command` with `args` as its argv (no shell involved) inside\n   * the sandboxd container, runs it to completion, and returns its stdout,\n   * stderr, and exit code. A non-zero exit code is a normal result, not a\n   * thrown error; an executable that cannot be found is rejected by\n   * sandboxd with a SandboxdRpcError (code \"not_found\"). For shell syntax\n   * (pipes, redirects, `&&`, globbing), invoke a shell explicitly:\n   * `run(\"sh\", [\"-c\", \"...\"])`. Requires the optional `@bufbuild/protobuf`,\n   * `@connectrpc/connect`, and `@connectrpc/connect-node` dependencies to be\n   * installed.\n   *\n        const started = await gen.process.start(spec, pty, streamSignal);\n        const classify = (err: unknown): unknown =>\n          this.classifyOperationFailure(\n            err,\n            gen,\n            undefined,\n            userSignal,\n            handleAbort.signal,\n          );\n        const session: ProcessSession = {\n          pid: started.pid,\n          pty: pty !== undefined,\n          next: async () => {\n            try {\n              return await started.events.next();\n            } catch (err) {\n              throw classify(err);\n            }\n          },\n          writeStdin: (data, callOpts) =>\n            this.processCall(gen, callOpts, (ms, signal) =>\n              gen.process.writeStdin(started.pid, data, ms, signal),\n            ),\n          signal: (sig, callOpts) =>\n            this.processCall(gen, callOpts, (ms, signal) =>\n              gen.process.sendSignal(started.pid, sig, ms, signal),\n            ),\n          resize: (size, callOpts) =>\n            this.processCall(gen, callOpts, (ms, signal) =>\n              gen.process.resizeTty(started.pid, size, ms, signal),\n            ),\n          cancel: () =>\n            handleAbort.abort(\n              new SandboxClosedError(\"Process handle is closed\"),\n            ),\n          onAbort: (cb) => {\n            const fire = (): void => cb(classify(streamSignal.reason));\n            if (streamSignal.aborted) {\n              fire();\n              return () => {};\n            }\n            streamSignal.addEventListener(\"abort\", fire, { once: true });\n            return () => streamSignal.removeEventListener(\"abort\", fire);\n          },\n        };\n        return new ProcessHandle(session, {\n          onStdout: opts?.onStdout,\n          onStderr: opts?.onStderr,\n        });\n      },\n    );\n  }\n\n  /** One unary RPC against the process's own connection generation. */\n  private processCall(\n    gen: ConnectionGeneration,\n    opts: ProcessCallOptions | undefined,\n    fn: (timeoutMs: number, signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<void>,\n  ): Promise<void> {\n    const timeoutMs = validateTimeoutMs(\"timeoutMs\", opts?.timeoutMs);\n    return this.runOperation(\n      timeoutMs,\n      opts?.signal,\n      (_gen, signal) => fn(timeoutMs, signal),\n      gen,\n    );\n  }\n\n  private async readFileImpl(\n    path: string,\n    opts?: FileCallOptions,\nimport { BRIDGE_READY } from \"./bridge.js\";\n\n/**\n * Lets one bridge cold-start at a time. A bridge process loads the Kubernetes and gRPC clients before it\n * can answer pi-env's hello, which takes about 12 s of wall time on the orchestrator's 250m CPU limit.\n * Several at once share that CPU, each takes several times longer, and pi-env gives up on a connection\n * that stays silent for 30 s (\"pi-env connection timed out\"). Serialising the starts keeps each one fast.\n */\nexport class StartGate {\n  readonly #maxHoldMs: number;\n  #tail: Promise<void> = Promise.resolve();\n\n  /** `maxHoldMs` frees the turn even if the holder never reports ready (a bridge that crashed early). */\n  constructor(maxHoldMs: number) {\n    this.#maxHoldMs = maxHoldMs;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Resolves on this caller's turn. The returned release hands the turn on; calling it again is a no-op. Aborting\n   * `signal` while waiting gives the turn up at once and rejects: a waiter that is gone must not take the gate later\n   * and hold it until `maxHoldMs`.\n   */\n  async acquire(signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<() => void> {\n    let release: () => void = () => undefined;\n    const mine = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {\n      release = resolve;\n    });\n    const previous = this.#tail;\n    this.#tail = previous.then(() => mine);\n    const cancelled = new Promise<never>((_resolve, reject) => {\n      const cancel = (): void => {\n        release();\n        reject(new Error(\"start gate wait cancelled\"));\n      };\n      if (signal?.aborted === true) cancel();\n      else signal?.addEventListener(\"abort\", cancel, { once: true });\n    });\n    // Nobody may be waiting on `cancelled` any more once `previous` won; it must not become an unhandled rejection.\n    cancelled.catch(() => undefined);\n    await Promise.race([previous, cancelled]);\n    const timer = setTimeout(release, this.#maxHoldMs);\n    timer.unref();\n    return () => {\n      clearTimeout(timer);\n      release();\n    };\n  }\n}\n\nexport interface GatedStart {\n  /** For `ConnectionOptions.command`: waits for the gate, then yields the bridge argv. */\n  command: () => Promise<readonly string[]>;\n  /** For `ConnectionOptions.onLog`: frees the gate when the bridge reports ready. */\n  onLog: (text: string) => void;\n  /** On close: frees the gate if this connection holds it, and cancels a turn it is still waiting for. */\n  release: () => void;\n}\n\n/** Wires one connection's bridge launches through `gate`; every respawn takes a new turn. */\nexport function gatedStart(gate: StartGate, argv: readonly string[]): GatedStart {\n  let release: (() => void) | undefined;\n  let waiting: AbortController | undefined;\n  const free = (): void => {\n    release?.();\n    release = undefined;\n  };\n  return {\n    command: async () => {\n      free();\n      const mine = new AbortController();\n      waiting = mine;\n      try {\n        release = await gate.acquire(mine.signal);\n      } finally {\n        if (waiting === mine) waiting = undefined;\n      }\n      return argv;\n    },\n    onLog: (text) => {\n      if (text.includes(BRIDGE_READY)) free();\n    },\n    release: () => {\n      // A command() still waiting for its turn must give it up, or it would take the gate after the close.\n      waiting?.abort();\n      free();\n    },\n  };\n}\n","isError":false}]}
ENTRY 12492 {"id":12492,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"[thinking]## Findings\n\n### 1. Remote output ignores local backpressure\n- Severity: high\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/sandbox-env/src/bridge.ts:64\n- What happens: If the bridge’s stdout or stderr consumer is slow or stops reading, remote output continues to be consumed and queued in the Node writable. A sufficiently large or continuous output can exhaust the bridge’s memory instead of slowing the remote process.\n- Evidence: `onStdout: (chunk) => void stdout.write(chunk)`\n- Fix idea: Propagate writable backpressure to remote output consumption, pausing reads until `drain`. This may require using an async output iterator rather than the SDK’s synchronous callbacks.\n\n### 2. Output errors during the final flush can be reported as success\n- Severity: medium\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/sandbox-env/src/bridge.ts:108\n- What happens: If the remote process exits successfully while stdout still has buffered data, a subsequent stdout write failure during the flush is no longer part of the race. The flush callback resolves even on error, so the bridge can return exit code 0 despite losing output.\n- Evidence: `if (outcome.kind === \"exit\") await flush(stdout);`\n- Fix idea: Make `flush` reject on its write callback’s error and handle that failure as a fatal bridge outcome.\n\n### 3. Stdout errors can occur before the error listener is installed\n- Severity: medium\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/sandbox-env/src/bridge.ts:63\n- What happens: The remote process can emit output while `options.start()` is still pending. If writing that output causes stdout to emit an error before the listener is registered, the error is unhandled and can terminate the bridge instead of following its fatal-error cleanup path.\n- Evidence: `proc = await options.start(... onStdout: (chunk) => void stdout.write(chunk) ...)`; the stdout error listener is installed only after the await.\n- Fix idea: Install stdout error handling before starting the remote process, and retain it through startup and cleanup.\n\nEND-OF-REPORT"}]}
